@title Using Class Map Query
@group developer

The PhutilClassMapQuery class is the primary tool for allowing code extension.

= How to Use =

The general pattern for making something extensible is:
1. Make an abstract class describing the generic capability, for example
  `TendToAnimal`. Define the API in this class, for example `function
  tend($animal)` and `function isApplicable($animal)`.
2. Define some methods in the base-class that are required for loading
3. Create static method(s) in the abstract class to load available
  implementations:

```lang=php
public static function loadAllAnimalTenders() {
  return id(new PhutilClassMapQuery())
    ->setAncestorClass(self::class)
    ->setUniqueMethod('getExtensionKey')
    // Many more features here
    ->execute();
}

final public function getExtensionKey() {
  return $this->getPhobjectClassConstant('EXTENSIONKEY');
}
```
4. Create one or two concrete implementations:

```lang=php
final class PetAnimal extends TendToAnimal {
  const EXTENSIONKEY = 'petanimal';

  public function isApplicable($animal) {
    return $animal != 'Jellyfish';
  }

  public function tend($animal) {
    echo "Pet pet $animal";
  }
}
```


5. Where you wish to use the feature, call `loadAll` and invoke the ones that
  make sense:

```lang=php

$tenders = TendToAnimal::loadAllAnimalTenders();
$animals = $this->getAllAnimals();

foreach ($animals as $animal) {
  foreach ($tenders as $tender) {
    if ($tender->isApplicable($animal)) {
      $tender->tend($animal);
    }
  }
}

```

`PhutilClassMapQuery` instantiates each subclass of `TendToAnimal`, returning
a list of concrete instances.

The queries are automatically cached and do not need to be wrapped in caching
logic.

An extension library can now provide new implementations of `TendToAnimal` by
simply sub-classing it, and it will be loaded and used automatically.

= Capabilities and Filters =

`PhutilClassMapQuery` supports several ways to filter, expand, and sort
instances.

- The `setUniqueMethod` is not technically required, but highly recommended.
  Adding one after the fact may require a database migration. using
  `getPhobjectClassConstant` is the current favorite pattern for implementing
  Unique methods.
- The `setExpandMethod` allows a single class to generate multiple instances.
  Useful for nearly-identical implementations, or for implementations that are
  based on configuration or available data.
- `setFilterMethod` allows an implementation to exclude itself from the result
  (for example, if it's part of a disabled application, or disabled by
  configuration).
- `setSortMethod` is useful for sorting the results.


= Common Patterns =

=== When Trying Different Things
Extensions are often used for different implementations of some operation, where
the right implementation depends on the environment.

In this case, implementations will be sorted by priority, and the calling code
will invoke them one at a time, until it succeeds or runs out of options.

Extensions can then add additional implementations to support other setups.

For example:
- [[ https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=PhabricatorFileStorageEngine |
  File Storage mechanism ]]
- [[ https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=PhabricatorDocumentEngine | File
  Rendering implementation ]] - the unique key also allows the user to
  explicitly select a renderer.
- [[ https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=AphrontSite | Sites selection ]]
  is used to resolve incoming HTTP requests to the right implementation.

=== When Showing Many Things
In some cases we just want to allow many different implementations of something
to be available at the same time, and either use them all or let the user choose
the one they want.

We'll then load all valid implementations and check each of them. Extensions can
easily add new options.

For example:
- [[ https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=HeraldAdapter | Herald Adapters
  ]] selection when creating a new Rule.
- [[ https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=PhabricatorDashboardPanelType |
  Dashboard Panels ]] selection.
- [[  https://we.phorge.it/dev/classtree/?symbol=PHUICurtainExtension | Curtain
  Extensions ]] add UI elements to the "Curtain" (meta-data section) of
  applicable object pages. All applicable extensions are used for each page.
- Applications, config options, Search extensions, and many more core features
  are using this pattern.
